bill and Eileen alley spoke to us about  the addiction that nearly killed their  son David the heroin started getting the  better of him  I kept calling the police on him hoping  the police would arrest him I spent many  days following him wondering where he's  at texting my husband go check on David  go make sure he's breathing checking on  him like he was a newborn child we take  turns getting up in the middle of night  make sure our son was still alive I  heard I heard those signs of you know  heroin the dance the slap the bouncing I  call it his heroin dance and I went down  and I said David I can't take it no more  I don't want to do it no more I  physically I'm not able to well he was  so high he cussed me out  he called me some names that he's never  ever called me and I knew at that moment  that I had to do something and I went up  and told his dad what had happened and I  went to my mom's for the night and I let  him handle it he said what do you want  nothing was going on and I pointed at  the door I said see that door I said get  out it I said don't come back until you  are off of that crap and he left  and the last time I seen him before he  overdosed again he was walking down the  driveway and he turned around and looked  at me and just smirked and said take a  good look it's the last time you're  gonna see me alive and he went and  stayed with some people that cared about  him he was there for not even two weeks  and he overdosed again they had they had  to kick their bathroom door in and do  CPR on him until the ambulance got there  they tell us EMS workers here in  Middletown Ohio saved their son's life  four times  he's now in rehab in Columbus and we're  told he's doing well we asked them about  a city councilman suggestion that EMS  may have to stop responding to calls  when patients overdose three times or  more even though they're down there  they're high on heroin they still  deserve to live they just don't know it  they're consumed by an addiction you  know there's people out there that don't  have any sympathy for people with  addictions and you know they believe the  narcan should be cut off after three  times all I can say is I hope they never  have to change their stance on it  because if they do that means some  things were something really bad has  happened to someone they love I do get  it I really do some of these stories are  very heart wrenching and I feel very  sorry for everybody or anybody who has  this issue but people need to understand  that I'm a city councilor my job as the  city councilor is to make sure this city  can continue to function and provide  services to its citizens and at the rate  we're going we've got to do something I  think I think we should save every life  we possibly can but what do we do when  we don't any money it angers me that's a  death sentence to thousands of people  thousands of people that's not the  answer to the drug problem there's not  the answer to our heroin epidemic  our town is in crisis full-blown crisis  we need help and taking away the narcan  it's not gonna help it's gonna make it  worse  the bodies are gonna add up the families  are gonna mourn these people need a  chance like my son head for CBS this  morning Tony to copal Middletown Ohio
 

